SINA_SMART["biophys1-interactions"] = {"lecture":"biophys1-interactions","title":"Radiation-matter interactions, detection and dosimetry","mapStatus":"auto: generated by universal.py from the lecture summary and key points; not human-reviewed","los":[{"id":"biophys1-interactions:e0","text":"Direct ionising: charged particles (α, β, p). Indirect: photons, neutrons.","topic":"The big picture","sec":0,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"biophys1-interactions:e1","text":"LET = energy lost per unit length; rises as particles slow.","topic":"Charged particles","sec":1,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"biophys1-interactions:e2","text":"Bragg peak: maximum dose at the end of range (protontherapy).","topic":"Charged particles","sec":1,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"biophys1-interactions:e3","text":"α: stopped by paper or skin; dangerous internally.","topic":"Charged particles","sec":1,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"biophys1-interactions:e4","text":"Electrons: ionisation, excitation, Bremsstrahlung (X-ray tube).","topic":"Charged particles","sec":1,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"biophys1-interactions:e5","text":"N = N₀e^(−µx); X½ = ln2/µ.","topic":"Photons","sec":2,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"biophys1-interactions:e6","text":"Photoelectric: photon absorbed by K electron; Compton: outer electron + scattered photon; pair production > 1.022 MeV.","topic":"Photons","sec":2,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"biophys1-interactions:e7","text":"Detector qualities: efficiency, background, energy resolution, dead time.","topic":"Detection and dosimetry","sec":3,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"biophys1-interactions:e8","text":"Scintillation (NaI + photomultiplier): gamma camera, PET.","topic":"Detection and dosimetry","sec":3,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"},{"id":"biophys1-interactions:e9","text":"Gas detectors: ionisation chamber, proportional counter, Geiger-Müller; dosimeters: film, TLD, OSL, electronic.","topic":"Detection and dosimetry","sec":3,"importance":"core","highYield":"insufficient-source"}],"vmap":{},"mlevel":{"uy7s42":"a","ef4o77":"a","ipi1yu":"a","4mxyfp":"a","yetdgh":"a","tiq9mp":"a","lhns7z":"a","1nwyeg0":"a","1oqa649":"a","1sj2zrj":"a","1e6u6af":"a","5io0mx":"a","10g3z1l":"a","4a00gw":"a","1ky5xg0":"a"},"mapCounts":{"verified":0,"auto":15,"ambiguous":0,"missing":0},"mrules":[],"items":[],"experimental":[],"status":{},"difficulty":{},"expl":{"biophys1-interactions:e0":{"s":0,"u":["Direct ionising: charged particles (α, β, p). Indirect: photons, neutrons.","Ionising radiations are directly ionising (charged particles: light β⁻, β⁺ electrons; heavy α, protons, fission fragments) or indirectly ionising (X and γ photons, neutrons)."],"dp":[]},"biophys1-interactions:e1":{"s":1,"u":["LET = energy lost per unit length; rises as particles slow.","Linear energy transfer (LET) = energy lost per unit length, LET ∝ q²nZ/v²: it rises as the particle slows; linear ionisation density (LID) = ion pairs per unit length (LET = W × LID)."],"dp":[]},"biophys1-interactions:e2":{"s":1,"u":["Bragg peak: maximum dose at the end of range (protontherapy).","The Bragg curve : small losses at first, rising as the particle slows, with a maximum (Bragg peak) just before it stops."],"dp":[{"h":"Why protontherapy spares healthy tissue","t":"X-ray beams deposit dose all along their path, including beyond the tumour. Protons deposit little energy on entry and most of it in the Bragg peak at a depth set by their energy, then stop. Tuning the energy puts the peak inside the tumour and leaves tissue behind it almost untouched, which matters near the spinal cord or in children. Source: the lecture's Bragg curve applications."}]},"biophys1-interactions:e3":{"s":1,"u":["α: stopped by paper or skin; dangerous internally.","Radiotherapy (protontherapy) tunes the energy to place the peak in the tumour, sparing healthy tissue. α particles are stopped by a few cm of air, paper or the skin surface: little external risk, but dangerous if inhaled or ingested ( internal exposure )."],"dp":[]},"biophys1-interactions:e4":{"s":1,"u":["Electrons: ionisation, excitation, Bremsstrahlung (X-ray tube).","Electrons : electron-electron collisions cause ionisation or excitation along a broken path; deflection by the nucleus produces braking X-rays (Bremsstrahlung) , used in the Coolidge X-ray tube and linear accelerators."],"dp":[]},"biophys1-interactions:e5":{"s":2,"u":["N = N₀e^(−µx); X½ = ln2/µ.","Half-value layer X½ = ln2/µ : each X½ halves the beam (N₀/2, /4, /8…)."],"dp":[]},"biophys1-interactions:e6":{"s":2,"u":["Photoelectric: photon absorbed by K electron; Compton: outer electron + scattered photon; pair production > 1.022 MeV.","Mechanisms: photoelectric effect (photon absorbed by a bound electron, mostly K shell, if its energy exceeds the binding energy; the photoelectron gets E − E_binding; then fluorescence X-rays or Auger electrons), Compton effect (on a loosely bound outer electron: the electron is ejected and a lower-energy photon is scattered), and materialisation (pair production: photon above 1.022 MeV → electron + positron).","Photons are attenuated exponentially: N = N₀·e^(−µx) , µ = linear attenuation coefficient (depends on material Z and density and photon energy; high-energy photons are less attenuated; dense, high-Z materials attenuate more)."],"dp":[{"h":"Why lead shields X-rays","t":"Photon attenuation increases with the density and atomic number of the absorber, especially through the photoelectric effect at diagnostic energies. Lead is dense and has a high Z, so a few millimetres cut the beam by several half-value layers, which is why aprons and walls in radiology use it. Source: the lecture's photon attenuation slides."}]},"biophys1-interactions:e7":{"s":3,"u":["Detector qualities: efficiency, background, energy resolution, dead time.","Characteristics: detection efficiency (intrinsic: detected/received; extrinsic: detected/emitted), background (count without a source: cosmic, telluric, detector materials, electronics), energy resolution (ability to separate close energies; NaI ≈ 5-10% at ⁹⁹ᵐTc 140 keV, semiconductors ≈ 1%) and dead time (e.g. ~200 µs for a Geiger-Müller tube)."],"dp":[]},"biophys1-interactions:e8":{"s":3,"u":["Scintillation (NaI + photomultiplier): gamma camera, PET.","Scintillation detectors : a NaI crystal emits visible light when excited, converted by photomultipliers into a signal: gamma camera (scintigraphy, SPECT), PET, whole-body counter (internal contamination)."],"dp":[]},"biophys1-interactions:e9":{"s":3,"u":["Gas detectors: ionisation chamber, proportional counter, Geiger-Müller; dosimeters: film, TLD, OSL, electronic.","Gas detectors : ions collected between electrodes; by voltage: recombination, ionisation chamber (dose calibrator, survey meter), proportional counter (surface contamination), limited proportionality, Geiger-Müller .","Individual dosimetry : passive (film, TLD thermoluminescent, OSL optically stimulated luminescence) and active electronic dosimeters with real-time reading."],"dp":[]}},"meta":{"uy7s42":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-interactions:e0/recall/1"},"ef4o77":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-interactions:e1/recall/1"},"ipi1yu":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-interactions:e2/recall/1"},"4mxyfp":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-interactions:e3/recall/1"},"yetdgh":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-interactions:e4/recall/1"},"tiq9mp":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-interactions:e6/recall/1"},"lhns7z":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-interactions:e5/recall/1"},"1nwyeg0":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-interactions:e5/recall/1"},"1oqa649":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-interactions:e6/recall/2"},"1sj2zrj":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-interactions:e6/recall/3"},"1e6u6af":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-interactions:e6/recall/4"},"5io0mx":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-interactions:e7/recall/1"},"10g3z1l":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-interactions:e8/recall/1"},"4a00gw":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-interactions:e7/recall/2"},"1ky5xg0":{"a":"recall","vg":"biophys1-interactions:e9/recall/1"}},"explRev":"c826436db41a"};
